    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Stalin developed the doctrine of socialism in one country from December 1924, holding that socialism could be completed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> without a spreading world revolution, and used it against Trotsky’s permanent revolution.</li><li>The Stalinist state carried out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a>, dekulakisation, the gulag system and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">mass deportations</a>; Soviet archives show nearly 700,000 executions in 1937 and 1938, and the emergent scholarly consensus is that around 1.5 to 1.7 million died in the gulag system from 1930 to 1953.</li><li>Historians disagree on whether Stalinism grew out of Leninism: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-pipes">Richard Pipes</a> called it the natural consequence of Leninism, while Roy Medvedev and Graeme Gill saw a break from it.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1936 Stalin told the journalist Roy W. Howard that the export of revolution was “nonsense”, yet a few years earlier he had turned the Soviet economy from the New Economic Policy to a planned economy in what became known as the Great Turn.</p></aside>
    </section>

