    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1924</span><span class="rf-text">Stalin first develops the idea of socialism in one country, elaborating it in his writings of 1925–26</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1930</span><span class="rf-text">The Soviet famine of 1930–1933 begins during forced collectivisation, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holodomor">Holodomor</a> follows near the end of 1932</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1936</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Terror</a> begins in September, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-yezhov">Nikolai Yezhov</a> heading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> Trials run to 1938</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1938</span><span class="rf-text">The History of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-the-soviet-union">Communist Party of the Soviet Union</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>) alters the history of the party and of its leaders</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1940</span><span class="rf-text">Trotsky is assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, the last of Stalin’s opponents among the former party leadership</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1941</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">Mass deportations</a> of whole peoples begin, and by 1949 nearly 3.3 million people are deported</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1953</span><span class="rf-text">Stalin dies on 5 March and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-stalinization">de-Stalinization</a> follows</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1956</span><span class="rf-text">Khrushchev’s Secret Speech condemns Stalin’s cult of personality at the Twentieth Party Congress</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin.jpg" alt="Joseph Stalin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StalinCropped1943.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Franklin D. Roosevelt Library Public Domain Photographs, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The word Stalinism came into prominence in the mid-1930s, when Lazar Kaganovich reportedly declared: “Let’s replace Long Live Leninism with Long Live Stalinism!” Stalin dismissed this as excessive and as feeding a cult of personality that he thought might later be used against him. The term is used for the period in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> as General Secretary of the Central Committee, from 3 April 1922 to his death on 5 March 1953; it is dated from 1927 in the Soviet Union and from 1944 to 1953 in the satellite states. Stalin avoided the term “Marxism-Leninism-Stalinism” but allowed others to use it.</p><p>After Lenin’s death Stalin entered the party’s theoretical debates with the idea of “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socialism">Socialism</a> in One Country”, bound up with factional struggles, above all against Trotsky. He first developed it in December 1924 and elaborated it in his writings of 1925–26. The other Bolshevik leaders had regarded the revolution of 1917 as the start of a world revolution, but it had not spread, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> had returned to bourgeois rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> members from Zinoviev and Kamenev to Rykov, Bukharin and Tomsky at first thought the idea next to blasphemy. It could not be imposed until Stalin came close to being autocratic ruler around 1929, when Trotsky had been exiled and Zinoviev and Kamenev expelled from the party.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="ideas">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Core Ideas</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Stalin’s doctrine held that socialism could be completed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> but that its final victory could not be guaranteed, because of the threat of capitalist intervention. He kept the Leninist view that world revolution was still necessary, and the Marxist belief that the state would wither away, but held that the Soviet state would remain until international <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> was finally defeated. The concept joined Marxist and Leninist ideas with nationalist ideals and served to discredit Trotsky, who promoted “permanent revolution”, as a defeatist. Stalin also argued that the proletarian state had to grow stronger before it could wither away, so that it could defeat counter-revolutionary elements.</p><p>At the start of the 1930s Stalin turned away from the mixed economy of the New Economic Policy (NEP) to a planned economy, in what became known as the Great Turn. Industrialisation was officially meant to speed development toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>, because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> lagged behind Western countries, and it came with mass collectivisation of agriculture and rapid urbanisation.</p><p>Orthodoxy was enforced in culture and science: socialist realism was imposed on the arts, Einstein’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theory-of-relativity">theory of relativity</a> was denounced as “bourgeois idealism”, and Trofim Lysenko’s pseudoscientific theories were favoured over genetics.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread &amp; Variants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/khorloogiin-choibalsan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khorloogiin-choibalsan.jpg" alt="Khorloogiin Choibalsan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/khorloogiin-choibalsan">Khorloogiin Choibalsan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Horloogiyn_Choybalsan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Stalinism was also the doctrine of the Soviet satellite states between 1944 and 1953, and it subordinated the interests of foreign communist parties to those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-the-soviet-union">Communist Party of the Soviet Union</a>, which it treated as the leading vanguard party of communist revolution. Its methods reached beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. Stalin sent a contingent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> operatives to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and set up a Mongolian version of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd-troika">NKVD troika</a>, and the Mongolian ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorloogiin-choibalsan">Khorloogiin Choibalsan</a> closely followed his lead. The Chinese warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheng-shicai">Sheng Shicai</a>, who had Communist leanings, invited Soviet intervention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> in the 1930s. Stalinist forces also oversaw purges of anti-Stalinist elements among the Spanish Republican insurgents during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>, among them the Trotskyist POUM faction and anarchist groups.</p><p>After Stalin’s death in 1953 Khrushchev repudiated his policies and condemned his cult of personality in the Secret Speech to the Twentieth Party Congress in 1956. Most communist parties that had adhered to Stalinism, except those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">German Democratic Republic</a> and the Socialist Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, abandoned it and adopted Khrushchev’s positions to a greater or lesser degree. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> chose to split from the Soviet Union, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, siding with it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-soviet-split">Sino-Soviet split</a>, remained committed, at least in theory, to its brand of Stalinism, Hoxhaism, for decades under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-hoxha">Enver Hoxha</a>. He later denounced Mao as a revisionist, along with almost every other self-identified communist organisation, which isolated Albania from the rest of the world.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="power">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">In Power</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-yezhov"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nikolai-yezhov.jpg" alt="Nikolai Yezhov" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-yezhov">Nikolai Yezhov</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ezhov_NI.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the 1930s Stalin consolidated nearly absolute power through a Great Purge of the party, in which those targeted were expelled, banished to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> labour camps or executed after trials by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd-troika">NKVD troikas</a>. After the assassination of the Leningrad party head Sergei Kirov, which Stalin may have orchestrated, investigations and trials widened. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Terror</a> of September 1936 to December 1938 was its peak, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-yezhov">Nikolai Yezhov</a> heading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> and quotas of arrests and executions set for each republic. Historians now estimate that nearly 700,000 people were executed in the terror (353,074 in 1937 and 328,612 in 1938), and scholars put the death toll of the Great Purge, with fatalities from imprisonment, at roughly 700,000 to 1.2 million.</p><p>Violence against the kulaks accompanied forced collectivisation; several international legislatures have declared it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>, and some historians dispute that. Several Western historians count Stalinist agricultural policy a key factor in the Soviet famine of 1930–1933, and some scholars believe the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holodomor">Holodomor</a>, from the end of 1932, was when it became an instrument of genocide; others dispute the intentionality.</p><p>From 1941 to 1949 nearly 3.3 million people were deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga-germans">Volga Germans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechens">Chechens</a>; by some estimates up to 43% of the resettled died of disease and malnutrition. By official Soviet estimates more than 14 million people passed through the gulags from 1929 to 1953, and the emergent scholarly consensus is that around 1.5 to 1.7 million died in the system from 1930 to 1953.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="critiques">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Critiques &amp; Debates</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/robert-conquest"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-conquest.jpg" alt="Robert Conquest" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/robert-conquest">Robert Conquest</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_Conquest_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rob C. Croes (ANEFO), CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Trotsky argued in The Stalin School of Falsification that the Stalinist faction routinely distorted political events, and claimed that a “river of blood” separated Stalin’s regime from Lenin’s. Trotskyists held that the Stalinist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> was neither socialist nor communist but a bureaucratised degenerated workers’ state. Max Shachtman called it bureaucratic collectivism, and Tony Cliff developed a critique of state <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>. Anarcho-communists criticised the party-state as bureaucratic.</p><p>On the economy, Fredric Jameson judged that Stalinism “modernized the Soviet Union”. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-conquest">Robert Conquest</a> disputed this, writing that its industrial advances could have come without collectivisation, famine or terror, and Stephen Kotkin called the case for collectivisation’s necessity “dead wrong”. Michael Ellman held that mass deaths from famine were not a “uniquely Stalinist evil”.</p><p>Historians divide on Leninism. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-pipes">Richard Pipes</a> considered Stalinism the natural consequence of Leninism, and Edvard Radzinsky and Stephen Kotkin likewise found continuity; Robert Service wrote that Lenin laid foundations, but that the passage to Stalinism “was not smooth and inevitable”. Roy Medvedev, Isaac Deutscher, Moshe Lewin and Graeme Gill saw a break, Gill adding that there is no agreement on what should constitute Stalinism. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleksandr-solzhenitsyn">Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn</a> argued that the term hides the effects of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> as a whole, and David L. Hoffmann argued that many of the state’s practices predated the Soviet regime.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mao-zedong.jpg" alt="Mao Zedong" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mao_Zedong_1950_Portrait_(3x4_cropped)(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chen Zhengqing (1917–1966), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In Western historiography Stalin is considered one of the worst and most notorious figures in modern history. Isaac Deutscher stressed the totalitarian character of Stalinism and its suppression of “socialist inspiration”, and several scholars have derided it for fostering anti-intellectual, antisemitic and chauvinistic attitudes in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. Historian David L. Hoffmann sums up the scholars’ consensus that the Stalin cult was a central element of Stalinism and one of the most salient features of Soviet rule.</p><p>Khrushchev’s Secret Speech of 1956 set off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-stalinization">de-Stalinization</a> and a relative liberalisation within the same political framework, and condemned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a>, most of which he reversed; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a>, Meskhetians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga-germans">Volga Germans</a> were not allowed to return in large numbers until 1991. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-soviet-split">Sino-Soviet split</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> declared Stalin 70% good and 30% bad. Maoists praised him for leading the Soviet Union and defeating fascism in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and criticised his view that class contradictions ended after the basic construction of socialism.</p><p>Historians have drawn other comparisons. Dietrich Schwanitz saw in Stalin “a monstrous reincarnation” of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a>, some reviewers have called Stalinism a form of red fascism, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> saw it as having turned Bolshevism into a Slavic fascism. Seumas Milne wrote in 2002 and 2006 that equating the evils of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> with those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazism</a> relativised the unique crimes of Nazism.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Stalinism is the term for the way <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> governed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> from 1927 to 1953: socialism in one country, forced industrialisation and collectivisation, a cult of personality and a police state. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a>, famines, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> and the gulags marked it, and historians still dispute how far it followed from Leninism.</em></p>

